  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nLooks quiet through Thursday as heights aloft rise. Weak \ninstability could yield a few pop-up showers or a storm \nWednesday afternoon, then again Thursday afternoon. No strong \nstorms are expected. A stronger shortwave trough is agreed upon \nThursday night through Friday. This will be the next chance for \nwidespread showers and some storms. Elevated mixed layer remains\nwell to the west though as shown by weaker mid-level lapse \nrates overhead. Thus, severe weather risk looks low. Some heavy \nrain potential centered on Friday with wave overhead (weaker \nwinds aloft) leading to slower storm motions, and since PWATs \nare up to 1.75 inches which is the 90th percentile. WPC has put \nthe majority of our area within a marginal excessive rainfall \nrisk (level 1 of 4), with isolated spots in central WI into a \nslight risk (level 2 of 4).\n\nAfter this wave pushes through, could still see some lingering \nshowers on Friday. High pressure building in this weekend \nresults in dry weather later Saturday into Sunday. \n\nNothing too notable in terms of temperatures. Highs rise into\nthe upper 80s to near 90 on Thursday, with a moderate heat risk\nshowing up as heat indices will only rise to around 90. As the\nupper trough is overhead on Friday with clouds and\nshowers/storms, temps cool into the upper 70s/lower 80s.\nTemperatures will remain similar for the weekend behind the\nsystem, then temperatures return back well into the 80s early to\nmid next week.",
